# Harmony AI App System Patterns
@Arch: Three-Tier Mobile (Presentation → Business Logic → Data)
- Background Layer: Persistent animated atmospheric background (Aurora/Nebula + Stardust) rendered behind the navigation stack in App.tsx. Uses absolute positioning with zIndex layering. Navigation contentStyle is transparent to allow the background to show through. Components use translucent/semi-opaque surfaces for glassmorphism effect.
- Screen Components: Full-page views, navigation, screen state
- Presentational Components: Pure display, props-driven, reusable
- Container Components: Data fetching, side effects, service connection
- State: Local (useState) → Context (theme/auth/emoji) → Redux/Zustand (future complex state)
- Navigation: Stack (primary), Modal (future)
- Build Flavors: Android productFlavors dev/prod via buildConfigField "boolean" IS_BETA; iOS dev/prod CI-driven (env regeneration + bundle ID override) — no scheme/xcconfig surgery
@Components:
- Screens: HomeScreen, ChatScreen, CharacterListScreen
- Services: EngineApiClient + engine repositories (management REST over loopback), engine event WebSocket, EngineHost/EngineService/EngineLifecycle, BackupOrchestrator, EmojiService, EntityEmojiActionService
- Storage: embedded engine database (via the loopback management API — NO app-side SQLite), AsyncStorage (settings/preferences), device Keychain/Keystore (secrets + DB key)
- Theme: Centralized via React Native Paper, Soulbits Engine palette
- Sync: engine↔engine changeset-v1 (engine-side; the app only configures it via DesktopSyncScreen). No app-side sync protocol.
@EmbeddedEngine:
- The app runs the Soulbits Engine ON DEVICE and is a thin client of it. ONE database, owned by the engine; the app keeps no schema and runs no migrations.
- Placement (unified behind `EngineHost`): Android = sidecar child process (`SidecarEngineHost`, ships `libengine.so` per ABI, exec from nativeLibraryDir, `ENGINE_READY` handshake, typed foreground service); iOS = in-process gomobile facade (`EmbeddedEngineHost`, `SoulbitsEngine.xcframework`, foreground tier via `EngineLifecycle` AppState start/stop). Both yield `{wsPort, wssPort, adminPort, token}`; `EngineService` routes through the selected host.
- Data access: every read/write goes over the engine's loopback management REST API (same contract the desktop Wails frontend uses) + engine event WebSocket. Auth = per-process loopback token (`X-Loopback-Token`); the app never embeds the admin key.
- DB-key custody: Android passes `SE_DB_KEY` to the sidecar; iOS provisions a 256-bit key into the Keychain (`EngineDbKey`) and hands it to the facade.
- iOS blind-tick lifecycle: `IOSLifecycleWakeHandler` (silent push → ensure-start → credential inject → tier-budget batch, 25s deadline inside the 30s window, degraded non-LLM path) + `IOSMaintenanceScheduler` (overnight BGProcessingTask: maintenance → batch → outreach relay → backup ride-along) + `PushRelayClient` (`POST /v1/lifecycle/notify`). The cloud is data-blind (keepalive cron + relay only).
- Outreach notification delivery (7-12): the engine events WS `GET /api/notifications/stream` (contract 1.4.0; `{type, entity_id, entity_name, message_id, title, body_preview, created_at, eligibility, delivery?}`) → `OutreachNotificationService` routes per AppState (foreground → in-app only; background/process-alive → local OS notification via iOS `SoulbitsNotifications` / Android `NotificationsModule` — per-entity channels + Android 13+ POST_NOTIFICATIONS). Engine gates stay authoritative (`eligibility != "eligible"` never notifies) and the shared short-window `outreachDedupe` (per `message_id`; 10 min / 512 ids) collapses engine-push / relay-push / local-post doubles. Settings: `notification_transport: auto|direct|cloud|off` + "Hide content on lock screen" (`NotificationSettings`, AsyncStorage `@harmony_setting_*`). iOS wake relay covers SYNC-applied outreach: `SyncAppliedOutreachCollector` subscribes during catch-up/batch and relays via `PushRelayClient`, gated by transport (`auto`/`cloud` only) + a persisted `OutreachRelayWatermark` (engine `created_at` high-water mark, mirrors the engine observer); the relay payload gains `messageId` (additive; cloud stays blind). Direct/custom-FCM (topology A): `push_token` in `INIT_ENTITY` session params (engine-targeted/direct sessions only — cloud conduct-proxy fenced) from `PushTokenStore` (SDK FCM token > native raw-APNs fallback). Firebase `@react-native-firebase/app` + `messaging@26.4.0` (modular API) acquires the FCM token → cloud `DeviceAuthService.registerDevice` + a directly-paired engine; runtime-guarded (placeholder config / SDK-less build degrades to the native seam, never crashes).
- Cloud account: SoulbitsCloud sign-in injects the PASETO as the `soulbitscloud` provider credential (inference direct); backup is user-owned-storage (encrypted snapshots to iCloud/WebDAV/Dropbox/OneDrive/local) with W1 account escrow. No cloud engine peer.
- Engine-contract gates: `src/services/engine/engineContract.ts` (`ENGINE_CONTRACT_VERSION` + `REQUIRED_ENGINE_ENDPOINTS`), typed wire fixtures, mapper conformance + loopback smoke suites, `scripts/check-engine-contract.mjs` + the opt-in `contract-drift` CI job. Canonical doc: `docs/engine-contract-gates.md`.
@Database (HISTORICAL — app-side TS SQLite replica DELETED in task 3-4; the engine owns the schema):
- The app's former on-device SQLite replica, its 47 mirrored migrations, the SQLite repository layer, and the schema-parity tooling are gone. What remains: `src/database/repositories/index.ts` is a pure re-export of the API-backed adapters under `src/services/engine/repositories/*` (no SQLite fallback).
- Outgoing-message authorship (D-35): the app renders its own outgoing messages as OPTIMISTIC in-memory pending rows (no SQLite mirror, no app-authored engine ids), reconciled against engine reads; incoming messages are driven by engine events.
- Mute/disable is engine-authoritative (`PUT /api/entities/:id` `is_muted`); the app-local AsyncStorage overlay is deleted.
- The engine migrations below (referenced in older notes) are engine-side and no longer mirrored here.
@EntityLifecycle (HISTORICAL — the app-side replica/sync machinery these rulings describe was DELETED in task 3-4; entity IDs, tombstones and GC are now engine-authoritative — see the engine repo's docs/Entity-Lifecycle.md and changeset sync):
- ID schema (D2/D22/D23, 2026-09-07): every entity id is DERIVED at creation as `{slug(name)}-{YYYYMMDDHHMMSS}` in UTC (valid-charset chars `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` pass through, other runs collapse to one `-`, edges trimmed, base cap 48, empty → `entity` — the 6-1 §1 vector rule; slug-rule prose contradiction resolved to the vectors). Ids are immutable for life; NO explicit-id creation and NO rename exist anywhere ("rename" = alias edit). Reserved names `user`/`deleted` throw typed `ReservedEntityNameError`. The app mints through ONE DB-backed seam `mintEntityId(name)` in `entities.ts` (D68: `deriveEntityId` → reserved check → ghost-aware `nextFreeDerivedId` backstop for same-second collisions, `-2`/`-3` suffixes); `mintPersonaIdentity(displayName)` adds alias dedupe (D56: alias = name verbatim when free, auto-suffix only on a live twin). Persona display names live in `alias` + profile name; persona edits NEVER touch the id (D14). All five creation seams (openCharacterChat, createPartner, createUserPersona, createUserPersonaFromCard, duplicateAIPartner) route through the seam — future seams get correctness by construction (the N3 incident class).
- Deletion = tombstone → GC, NO restore (D1 as amended by D69–D78): soft-delete (tombstone) is the delete-propagation protocol; each side's sync-finalize GC purges tombstones older than the finalizing session's start over the unified 35-table allowlist (`GC_TABLES` == `SYNC_TABLES` == engine `registeredSyncTables`, pinned by `syncGcTablesParity.test.ts` against a hard-coded 35 list; child-first dependency order per D71(a)). Deletion is final once propagated + GC'd — recreation (fresh timestamped id, fresh greeting) is the only path back (D75); ids are never reused, guaranteed by construction (timestamped derivation), not by tombstone retention. Entity delete cascades to 8 children (interactions, conversation_messages, memories, emotion_state, lifecycle_state, entity_emoji_actions, chat_conversation_settings, entity_module_mappings) under ONE shared `now` (D26/D17) so whole families purge predictably; `chat_conversation_settings` deletes are fully soft (D18: `deleted_at IS NULL` on all five read predicates + `deleted_at = NULL` resurrect in `upsertSettings`' ON CONFLICT). The `permanent` hard-delete parameter was fully REMOVED from all repository delete signatures (user ruling 2026-09-06) — hard deletes do not exist app-side.
- Known tombstone-protocol exception (D67): engine memory consolidation hard-deletes source memories (no tombstone propagates) — app devices retain consolidated source memories indefinitely; the app-local orphan sweep (`cleanupOrphanedMemories`, kept per D77) only removes memories whose referencing interactions are gone. Documented accepted divergence; revisit only if user-visible.
- Sync version gate (D6/3-3): app always sends `sync_schema_version: 2`; an engine advertising < 2 (absent field = v1) or rejecting with `reason=unsupported_schema_version` enters sticky `serverUpdateRequired` — ONE choke point (`initiateSync()` short-circuits at its top while sticky, covering ALL sync triggers), `scheduleReconnect()` is a no-op while sticky (no handshake loop), and a ~10-min slow re-probe (`SERVER_UPDATE_REPROBE_INTERVAL_MS`) re-handshakes until the engine updates, then the app re-pulls and converges automatically. The read-only floating-chat provider mirrors the gate.
- One-time wipe/rebuild convergence (D11/D61/D58/D19): this update performs a one-time local wipe + full re-pull from the engine (accepted loss: local-only rows never synced). The wipe flag is consumed in the BOOT window (`DatabaseContext.initializeDb` via `runWipeRebuildIfPending` — before any screen renders; never inside `initiateSync`); the labeled "Rebuilding from Soulbits Engine…" gate shows for the WIPE duration; id-keyed AsyncStorage preferences (impersonated persona, reply-mode keys, legacy prefs) reset once; the first post-wipe `initiateSync` escalates to `force_full_sync: true` via the cleared-watermark mechanism. Placeholder migrations 000045/000046 are comment-only TS no-ops for cross-repo numbering parity.
- Rebuild-on-purge-floor (D76/4-5): `SYNC_REJECT reason=rebuild_required` (device watermark below the engine's `max_purged_deleted_at` purge floor) → abort sync → persist wipe flag (BEFORE restarting — crash-safe) → `react-native-restart` process restart (D82; new dependency v0.0.29); boot then runs the D61 wipe naturally. In-process loop guard: a re-flag after a completed rebuild logs + surfaces a diagnostic, never restart-loops. Version gate takes precedence (no rebuild signal can arrive while sticky). Fresh installs are exempt engine-side.
- Sync failure surfacing (D13/4-2): sync confirm payloads carrying structured `error_code` (`sync_conflict` or `apply_failed`) → typed `SyncConflictError` (old engines → plain `Error`); `syncAndWait({critical: true})` rejects on the whole failure class (`sync:error`/`sync:rejected`/`sync:aborted`) and upgrades the SHARED wait (D34). Chat open runs a critical wait whenever the entity is dirty (`entity.updated_at > last full-sync watermark`, D55) so sync fully executes BEFORE INIT_ENTITY — resolving only on a COMPLETE round incl. bounded ≤2 re-check rounds; clean entities skip the wait (zero latency).
- Failed-session UX (D36/4-3): failed sessions are FLAGGED (`failed` marker on `InteractionSession`, retained in service + context maps), never silently deleted (own-entity disconnect flags + emits `session:error`, D65); ChatDetail shows an error banner, red `error` connection dot, revealed splash with Retry/Back; INIT_ENTITY failures classify via structured `error_code` (D35: `entity_not_defined` → bounded retries + "Sync now" hint; `entity_disabled`/`entity_exists_deleted` terminal — deletion is final; string fallback for old engines). Flagged entries clear on navigation-back or the next `session:started`; `closeAllSessions` (background/sync-loss) keeps its deliberate full teardown as the GC point.
@Decisions (HISTORICAL — the app-side sync name-clash/estimate/wipe machinery below was DELETED in task 3-4; sync is now engine↔engine changeset-v1):
- Sync name-clash resolution: an incoming module-config record sharing a `name` with a different-id local record would violate the DB-level UNIQUE name constraint and abort the whole sync transaction — so `applyBufferedSyncData` first runs clash detection (`syncNameClash.ts`, pure): `buildNameClashPlan` over buffered server records vs local rows on the 8 `NAME_UNIQUE_TABLES` (backend/cognition/movement/rag/stt/tts/vision/imagination configs), plus `CONFIG_ID_REFERENCES` for records referencing provider_config_id / character_profile ids. The UI (SyncSettingsScreen, `sync:name_clash` event) picks ONE resolution applied per clash or "Apply to all": **overwrite** = server wins (adopt server id+values, remap local refs), **keep** = app wins (adopt server id, KEEP local values, remap refs, re-send), **rename** = local keeps its id renamed `X (ts)`, server record inserted. Decisions are per-sync-session (never carried into the next sync). All 8 module-config tables enforce `name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` (migration 000034 rebuilt vision/imagination; the other 6 already had it).
- Keep ID-cascade (module config → provider config): "keep" must preserve the COMPLETE app setup and just match IDs to the incoming data. The keep directive carries `providerRefs: ReadonlyArray<KeepProviderRefCapture>` (`{configColumn, providerColumn, localProviderConfigId, localProvider}`) captured PRE-TRANSACTION in `buildApplyPlan` from `clash.localRecord`. `applyIdAdoption` then (a) adopts the server id into the local module-config row bumping `updated_at` and re-pointing each provider ref column to the SERVER provider id, (b) RENAMES the local `provider_config_*` row to the server provider-config id via `cascadeKeepProviderConfig` (values + created_at preserved, updated_at bumped) instead of merging onto the server row — no orphaned local rows, nothing to clean up; the incoming server provider duplicate is SKIPPED (added to `keptProviderServerIds`) so the rename can't collide on PK. Post-commit, keep-resolved module + `keepCascadedProviderKeys` are REMOVED from `serverRecordIds` so the kept records re-send to the engine. All cascade SQL runs SYNCHRONOUSLY inside the tx — callback-nested executeSql in the test harness (NodeDatabase) executes after COMMIT and silently breaks atomicity.
- Provider-reference columns are PER-TABLE, not uniform: `PROVIDER_CONFIG_REFERENCES` maps every module-config table to its `{configColumn, providerColumn}` pair(s) — the 7 standard tables use `provider_config_id`/`provider`, but `stt_configs` has TWO (`transcription_provider_config_id`/`transcription_provider` AND `vad_provider_config_id`/`vad_provider`; verified via PRAGMA table_info on the device DB — a naive regex over schema SQL falsely matched the `…_provider_config_id` substring). Never hardcode `provider_config_id` for stt_configs; verify schema against a live DB (`PRAGMA table_info`), not grep.
- Size-estimate confirmation gate: the ENGINE blocks after `SYNC_DATA_SIZE_ESTIMATE` until the app replies `SYNC_DATA_SIZE_ESTIMATE_CONFIRM` (`{sync_session_id, event_id, status: SUCCESS|REJECTED}`, 60s timeout → SYNC_REJECT + abort; no data without approval). App keeps `pendingSizeEstimate` in SyncService and emits `sync:estimate`; the UI layer (SyncConnectionContext) decides whether to prompt or auto-confirm via the pure gate `shouldPromptForSyncEstimate({totalRecords, imageCount, estimatedDownloadMB, limitMB, isInitialSync})` (`src/contexts/syncEstimateHelper.ts`): prompts only when `estimated_download_mb > limit` (default 5 MB via `ConnectionStateManager` key `sync_estimate_limit_mb`; `null` = "Unlimited" → never prompt), empty (0/0) auto-confirms, and `isInitialSync` (no persisted last-sync watermark) ALWAYS prompts for any non-empty estimate. `SyncSettingsScreen` exposes a 5/10/20/50/100/Unlimited dropdown (shared `SelectPicker`).
- Wire-format truth: the engine's payloads use snake_case JSON tags (`estimated_download_mb`, `total_records`, `image_count`, `includes_images`, `image_size_mb`). The app MUST read those exact keys — a PascalCase read silently yields 0/0/0 and disables the feature. Regression guard: integration test asserts the PascalCase key is `undefined`.
- Connection-error toast gate: `shouldShowConnectionErrorToast({isTransportSettled, isReconnecting, reconnectAttempt, isCertFlowActive})` (`syncSettlementHelper.ts`) — only the very first failure on a SETTLED transport with no pending cert decision may toast. Suppresses "connection error"/"connection dropped" spam while the pairing handshake / cert verification is in progress (the cert modal is the intended UX there). `isCertFlowActiveRef` set on `cert:verification_failed` + before TLS dials, cleared on settled connect; `ConnectionSetupScreen` mirrors `showCertModal` via a ref (effect closures go stale).
- Provider nesting for themed dialogs: `AppAlertProvider` AND `AppToastProvider` are mounted ABOVE `SyncConnectionProvider` in `App.tsx` so `useAppAlert()` (obsidian-glass themed modal) and `useToast()` (branded glass-pill toast) are available inside SyncConnectionContext. Reorder-safe because `AppAlertModal`/`AppToastView` only depend on ThemeContext. Listeners registered in `[]`-deps effects must capture `showAlert`/`showToast` through ref mirrors (this replaced the old OS-native `ToastAndroid.show` on Android).
- Settled-transport sync gate: auto-sync is DEFERRED until the sync transport is settled — `isSyncTransportSettled(connMode, persistedMode)` (src/contexts/syncSettlementHelper.ts): TLS (secure/insecure-ssl) + cloud always settled; `unencrypted` settled ONLY if the user explicitly persisted it; undefined/unknown → not settled. `SyncConnectionContext.handleSyncConnected` is async and skips `initiateSync()` on a provisional connection. Prevents (a) sensitive data crossing plaintext ws:// during pairing and (b) the ws→wss upgrade tearing down an in-flight sync (orphaned 'in_progress' session).
- Sync-abort safety net: `SyncService.abortSync(reason)` clears currentSession/syncPhase/incomingDataBuffer/serverRecordIds, rejects pending SYNC_DATA confirmation, emits `sync:aborted` (UI resets spinner). Wired to `disconnected:sync`, `error:sync`, and `sync:connection_replaced`. Buffered server data is DISCARDED (not applied); last-sync timestamp not advanced → engine re-sends everything next sync (lossless recovery).
- Teardown-signal-before-teardown: `ConnectionManager.disconnectConnection` removes listeners BEFORE disconnect (suppresses the WS 'disconnected' event on deliberate teardown). Any mid-session cleanup on replacement needs a dedicated pre-teardown signal — `createConnection` emits `sync:connection_replaced` (id==='sync' only) before `disconnectConnection(id)`.
- Per-source sync watermark: lastSync is stored per `SyncSource` (`selfhosted`/`cloud`) in AsyncStorage keys `last_sync_timestamp:<source>`. `getCurrentSource()` reads `connection_mode`. NO legacy global key (removed 2026-08-02). `clearAllLastSyncTimestamps()` (called by full-DB-wipe flows `wipeDatabaseCompletely`/`clearDatabaseData`) removes ALL per-source keys so a wipe starts from a clean initial state (next sync = full pull).
- CloudSessionStatus model: idle|requesting|provisioning|ready|failed. No `active` at service level — WS connectivity is separate (`useSyncConnection().isConnected`). `canUseChat` predicate: cloud→ready, self-hosted→isPaired. `connectionStatus` helper produces mode-aware labels for UI.
- Async polling flow: POST /v1/session/connect → 202 provisioning → poll at retry_after_ms → 200 ready → open WS. Max 95 polls (~190s). 503 failed = terminal.
- Consecutive WS failure counter (5): triggers fresh broker re-provision via cloudSessionService.connect(). Token-expiry pre-check before every WS dial. BROKER_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS client-side hack removed — stale-session detection delegated to failure counter + broker lifecycle.
- Coupled release: backend + app deploy together. POST /connect response contract changes (409→202, 200→202-provisioning, failed→503). No API versioning.
- React Native CLI (not Expo): Full native control for Executorch/messengers
- React Native Paper: Material Design 3 consistency
- React Navigation: Type-safe stack navigation
- TypeScript strict: Type safety, better DX
- Hooks → Redux/Zustand: Monitor complexity, add when needed
- SQLite: Local data persistence, Soulbits Engine schema compatible
- Offline queue: Queue operations when offline, process when online
@Patterns:
- Service Layer: All API calls centralized
- Queue-based Offline: Detect connection → queue ops → process when online
- Optimistic UI: Update UI immediately, sync async, handle conflicts
- Virtualized Lists: FlatList for performance
- Secure Storage: EncryptedStorage for sensitive data
- Error Boundaries: Graceful error handling
@Performance: FlatList virtualization, image lazy loading/caching, memoization, clean listener management.
@Security: HTTPS only, token auth, encrypted storage, no credentials in code.
@ProviderArchitecture:
- Provider registry (v0.4.5, workstream `.current_work/provider-registry/` R1–R12/A1–A11): the per-module `provider_config_*` world is RETIRED. Credentials live in ONE shared synced `provider_accounts` table (engine migration 000052): `type` (13 surviving — openai, openaicompatible, openrouter, anthropic, google, xai, mistral, ollama, localai, comfyui, elevenlabs, soulvoice, soulbitscloud), user `name`, `api_key`, `base_url`, typed `extra` JSON (openai org/project, xai team, comfyui auth + comfyui_mode, soulbitscloud auth-mode…). Competitors kindroid/kajiwoto/characterai are HARD-removed everywhere (R9; the kajiwoto legacy URL shim fully dropped per A6). Per-module behavior lives in a `capability_json` overlay on the 8 module-config tables; `provider_config_id`/`*_provider_config_id` columns are gone app-side — forms write flat `{provider, provider_account_id, capability_json}` bodies (no row minting).
- App seams: `src/services/engine/repositories/providers.ts` targets the accounts management API (`listAccounts/getAccount/create/update/delete`; DELETE 409 `provider_in_use` carries `referencing_configs` and is the authoritative in-use check); `EngineApiClient` += `verifyProvider` (stateless key check → 6-state VerifyResult: valid/invalid/throttled/blocked/scope_limited/unreachable + payload extras), `listProviderModels` (models + voices, engine-side module filter; engine-embedded models.dev catalog, offline-first, capability-gated emission), `getProviderCapabilities`. Engine-contract canary 1.5.0 → **1.6.0** (REQUIRED_ENGINE_ENDPOINTS + fixtures + conformance/loopback gates updated in 6-1).
- UI: Settings → **Providers** (`ProvidersScreen` + `accountFieldSchemas.ts` + shared `AccountForm`): accounts grouped by type, masked key, in-use badge, "Test key" verify chips (auto-runs after save), delete-409 alert listing referencing configs. api_key is returned UNMASKED by the engine (single-user local engine — deliberate; enables client-side re-verify). Module-config forms are a flow: Provider → Account (auto-collapses when single; "Add account…" opens AccountForm) → Capability (catalog-backed pickers with "Fetch from provider" live refresh + provenance chips detected/known/assumed + Custom… fallback; preset dropdown; param warnings hidden in simple mode, chip in expert mode, NEVER hard-blocked) → Module. STT keeps dual sub-forms (transcription + VAD) with PER-SLOT provider lists: transcription = openai/openaicompatible/openrouter/soulbitscloud/elevenlabs + soulvoice (A11) + localai + ollama (experimental, STT only — stock Ollama has NO TTS/VAD per R-AUDIO); VAD = soulbitscloud + soulvoice ONLY (the two with real VAD clients).
- Preset semantics (A3): presets supply DEFAULTS; explicit local values override (engine truth, providers/openai.go). UI labels accordingly — a preset never clobbers an explicit value. Presets ride the sampling_preset_name + extra_params columns (migration 021) via `usePresets` + `SamplingPresetSelect` in the capability section; extra params stay JSON TEXT edited as key-value pairs (typical_p, tfs, dry_*, xtc_* et al).
- Seeding division (A9): the ENGINE's `createDefaultData` (reworked engine-side in 3-1) stays the structural seeder on empty DBs (soulbitscloud account + a default config per applicable module — 7 configs; chat default `qwen-35-9b` per A10). The APP's ensure-flow (6-4) fills the soulbitscloud account PASETO on EVERY auth/re-auth/token-renewal (ungated; `auth:changed` + bootstrap) and gap-fills deleted seed configs ONE-SHOT (incl. the STT VAD slot → soulbitscloud/`silero-vad` per A8) — never overwrites. Supersedes the old create-only `injectSoulbitsToken`/`startSoulbitsTokenSync` pattern (its sync-push machinery died with 3-4); the engine still discriminates PASETO-mode by the `v4.local.` prefix on the account api_key.
- Account-scoped overlay refs: model/voice ids inside `capability_json` are meaningful RELATIVE to the linked account (ElevenLabs voices are per-account; local servers expose only installed models; xAI retired slugs silently redirect) — switching accounts triggers availability revalidation; stale refs are marked with re-fetch/replace UX, never silently dropped.
- Reactivated providers (6-5): mistral (backend/cognition/movement/vision/rag — NO stt/tts; Voxtral out of engine scope, future work), ollama (same set + stt experimental), localai (same set as openaicompatible); owed schema fields: google thinking_level/thinking_budget, xai image_model, mistral random_seed, ollama full research option set.
- Live-catalog contract (still true): hardcoded model fallbacks/defaults must reference real catalog/live entries; the 2-1 interim `providerModelCatalog.ts` constant was deleted in 6-3 once the engine capabilities endpoint landed.
@InteractionSession:
- Replaces DualEntitySession pattern: sessions are scoped to interaction IDs (not entity-pair IDs)
- InteractionSession interface: interactionId, interaction, participantIds, ownEntityId, connections Map (entityId → connection info), pendingTranscriptions Map
- Events keyed by interactionId: 'session:started', 'session:stopped', 'message:received', 'typing:indicator', etc.
- Participant-agnostic: supports N participants per session (world/private/group)
- Navigation adapted: ChatDetailScreen uses interactionId for session lookup instead of partnerEntityId
- Participant key derivation: private (sorted pair "entityA+entityB"), group (sorted join of all IDs), world (empty string)
- Scope derivation: 0-1 participants → world, 2 → private, 3+ → group
@EmojiArchitecture:
- Emoji Sets: native (platform default), twemoji (Twitter sprite), noto (Google sprite) — selected via EmojiContext
- Emoji Picker: Modal (full-screen, category tabs, search) and Inline (compact for chat input)
- Emoji Data: Pre-built emoji data service (EmojiService) loading from bundled data, category-based organization
- Emoji Actions: Entity-level emoji→action mappings stored in emoji_actions table
  - Action types: trigger custom emotion effects on emoji send
  - Emotion effects: Ekman8 emotion deltas (joy/sadness/trust/disgust/fear/anger/surprise/anticipation)
  - Resolution: EmojiService scans outgoing message text, resolves emoji → actions → AdditionalEffects payload → sent with SEND_MESSAGE
- Emoji-Aware Text: EmojiAwareText segments text into emoji/text spans; EmojiText renders with proper sprite caching
- DB: Migrations 22-23 via emoji_actions repository with soft delete pattern
@SessionResume:
- Session resume flow: INIT_ENTITY response includes `resumed: true` flag on Soulbits Engine side
- EntitySessionService checks resumed flag; if true, avoids re-creating connection and reconciles pending state
- Dangling event sync: On reconnect, backend refires any events that were queued while disconnected
- Reply mode: EntitySession passes `reply_mode` as capability during INIT_ENTITY; ChatPreferencesService stores per-partner preference
@AtmosphericBackground:
- DynamicAtmosphericBackground: 5 large radial-gradient-like animated blobs (LinearGradient circles) with independent Lissajous motion paths, prime-number cycle durations (31-53s), and oscillating opacity/scale. Theme-driven color extraction (accent.primary = Neon Magenta, accent.secondary = Indigo). Base overlay + vignette layers anchor edges in deep blue-black.
- StardustParticles: 40 deterministic pseudo-random particles (mulberry32 seed=42) with unique drift directions, twinkle periods (2-8s), and gentle floating motion (~30px drift). Rendered above the aurora but below the app UI.
- Layering: backgroundLayer (zIndex:0, absoluteFill) → foregroundLayer (zIndex:1, flex:1) in App.tsx. All native driver animations for 60fps performance.
@Glassmorphism:
- ThemedCard: Single universal card component. Obsidian Glass standard — translucent fill (45-55% opacity via hexToRgba of surface/elevated hex with theme.colors.glass.cardOpacity), 1dp hairline specular gradient border (borderGradientStart: silver/white top-left → borderGradientEnd: muted neon purple/indigo bottom-right), ambient radiant glow shadow (shadowColor: accent.primary, shadowOpacity: glass.glowOpacity 0.06-0.10, shadowRadius: glass.glowRadius 12-15dp). Optional left 3px accent stripe and top-left prismatic tint overlay.
- GlassTokens: cardOpacity (0.45-0.55 for dark themes, 0.85 for light), glowOpacity (0.06-0.10), glowRadius (10-15dp), borderGradientStart (rgba white), borderGradientEnd (rgba accent). All 9 themes carry per-theme glass values.
- Consumers: ThemedCard (primary), ThemeCard (settings), LandingCard, ThemedButton (secondary glass inner), ThemedAppbar (glass header). All read theme.colors.glass directly — no hardcoded opacity values.
- Dynamic Atmospheric Background bleeds through translucent cards, creating the "frosted glass" effect without backdrop-filter (not available in React Native).
